1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,324
Today, I want to talk about self-regulatory focus theory and how the actions of

2
00:00:07,324 --> 00:00:12,340
leaders can affect the way followers approach different situations.

3
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:18,320
Self-regulatory focus theory is a theory developed by Tori Higgins.

4
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:25,430
He says that a person's focus at any given time is to either approach pleasure

5
00:00:25,430 --> 00:00:27,720
or avoid pain.

6
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:32,295
These are two basic motivations that each and every one of us has,

7
00:00:32,295 --> 00:00:37,150
and they cause us to have different kinds of goals.

8
00:00:37,150 --> 00:00:43,540
Promotion goals in different life situations emphasise achievement.

9
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:50,430
Prevention goals are oriented towards the avoidance of punishment.

10
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:57,990
In a specific situation, our thoughts might focus more on promotion goals

11
00:00:57,990 --> 00:01:01,760
or more on prevention goals.

12
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:08,366
The theory suggests that two factors affect which goals we are focusing on.

13
00:01:08,366 --> 00:01:12,000
First, there is a chronic factor.

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,026
This factor is connected to a person's personality and says that each person has

15
00:01:19,026 --> 00:01:24,045
a basic tendency to either focus more on promotion goals

16
00:01:24,045 --> 00:01:30,570
or focus more on prevention goals as part of his or her personality.

17
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:36,923
Second, there is a situational factor which means that the context we are in

18
00:01:36,923 --> 00:01:42,369
can make us more likely to focus on one set of goals

19
00:01:42,369 --> 00:01:43,730
or the other.

20
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:48,350
For example, we are more likely to be thinking about pleasure

21
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:53,810
and to have promotion goals when we are spending time with a friend.

22
00:01:53,810 --> 00:02:00,823
In contrast, if we are working on an important project for our boss, we

23
00:02:00,823 --> 00:02:07,836
are more likely to try to avoid making mistakes and therefore have more prevention

24
00:02:07,836 --> 00:02:09,840
goals in our mind.

25
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:16,774
Research has shown that the goals we are focusing on at a given time

26
00:02:16,774 --> 00:02:19,250
affect the way we think.

27
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:20,560
For example,

28
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:29,730
when focusing on promotion goals, people consider their ideal self, their aspirations and gains.

29
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:33,250
They don't think about what they can lose,

30
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:36,330
so they think in a happier mode.

31
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:39,520
They feel more inspired to change.

32
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:46,300
When people are focusing on prevention goals, they think about their "ought" self.

33
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,900
What are they supposed to be?

34
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:51,550
What are people expecting from them?

35
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:55,400
They consider their obligations to others.

36
00:02:55,400 --> 00:03:06,889
As a result, they experience more anxiety and try to avoid situations where they

37
00:03:06,889 --> 00:03:08,530
could lose.

38
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:15,007
Now that I have talked about the two focuses and how they affect people,

39
00:03:15,007 --> 00:03:20,558
I want to look at the idea that the wav leaders behave,

40
00:03:20,558 --> 00:03:27,035
or their style of leading, can affect the focus that followers adopt in a

41
00:03:27,035 --> 00:03:27,960
specific situation.

42
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:37,340
In talking about leadership, we often mention transformational leaders and transactional leaders.

43
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:38,725
Transformational leaders,

44
00:03:38,725 --> 00:03:44,960
when interacting with their followers, focus on their development.

45
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:51,260
In their words and actions transformational leaders highlight change.

46
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:57,200
Their speech is passionate and conveys a definitive vision.

47
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:03,710
All of these things can encourage followers to think about what could be.

48
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:10,910
In other words, they inspire a promotion focus in their followers.

49
00:04:10,910 --> 00:04:20,312
In contrast, transactional leaders focus on developing clear structures that tell their followers exactly

50
00:04:20,312 --> 00:04:23,670
what is expected of them.

51
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:30,466
While they do explain the rewards people will get for following orders, they emphasise

52
00:04:30,466 --> 00:04:38,233
more how a follower will be punished or that a follower won't get rewarded if his

53
00:04:38,233 --> 00:04:40,660
or her behaviour doesn't change.

54
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:46,580
In short, they emphasise the consequences of making a mistake.

55
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:54,430
This emphasis will clearly lead followers to focus on avoiding punishment and problems.

56
00:04:54,430 --> 00:04:58,920
This is clearly a prevention focus.

57
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:05,702
In conclusion, it is important to understand that one focus is not necessarily better

58
00:05:05,702 --> 00:05:07,640
than the other one.

59
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:14,100
For a designer who works in a field where a lot of innovation is

60
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:17,330
needed, a promotion focus is probably better.

61
00:05:17,330 --> 00:05:23,082
In contrast, a prevention focus which causes people to work more cautiously

62
00:05:23,082 --> 00:05:29,792
and produce higher quality work might be very appropriate for a job like a

63
00:05:29,792 --> 00:05:31,230
surgeon, for example.

64
00:05:31,230 --> 00:05:38,238
The main point of the research, though, is that the actions of leaders can

65
00:05:38,238 --> 00:05:44,746
greatly influence whether people approach a situation with more of a promotion focus

66
00:05:44,746 --> 00:05:47,750
or more of a prevention focus.
